# Cross-platform hardware doctor

Collects evidence with operating-system tools instead of guessing from generic
error messages. Linux uses tools such as `lscpu`, `journalctl`, `lspci`,
`ubuntu-drivers`, `ip`, and `upower`; macOS uses `system_profiler` and `pmset`;
Windows uses CIM, PnP, and networking PowerShell cmdlets.

> **Session warning:** Linux display repair defaults to
> `--display-action restart`, which restarts the active display manager and
> ends graphical sessions. Save work before applying it.

## Actions

### `diagnose`

Choose `--area all`, `cpu`, `memory`, `display`, `storage`, `network`, or
`battery`. Missing optional commands are skipped. Storage health is handled by
the sibling `disk` command so filesystem and SMART behavior stays in one place.
Diagnosis is best-effort: failed native probes are printed but do not make the
overall diagnosis command fail.

### `fix`

Fixes are deliberately narrow:

- Linux display: restart the configured display manager, run
  `ubuntu-drivers install`, or reconfigure GDM3.
- Network: flush the native DNS cache.
- Storage: delegate verification and repair to `disk fix`.

There is no generic safe software repair for failed processors, memory,
batteries, or other physical parts. Diagnosis provides evidence for vendor
tests or replacement.

The former `gdm3-error-fix` workaround is not bundled. Its useful diagnosis
and signed-driver goal are covered by guarded native commands without changing
the selected display manager, sleeping, or forcing a reboot.

Dashboard previews work for every repair. GDM3 reconfiguration and any repair
that needs an interactive administrator password must be applied from the
displayed direct command; dashboard elevation works only when sudo/doas policy
already permits a non-interactive command.

## Direct CLI

```sh
hardware diagnose --area all
hardware diagnose --area display
hardware fix --area network
hardware fix --area display --display-action drivers --apply
hardware fix --area storage --target /dev/sdb1 --apply
```

Nothing mutates without `--apply`. Direct applies require typing `APPLY`; the
shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and then supplies `--apply --yes`.

## Exit status

`0` means diagnosis completed or a repair plan ran; diagnosis remains
best-effort when individual probes fail. `2` means the request, platform,
dependency, privilege, or repair operation failed.
